A||||8||fierospeeder||Concrete paint, garage||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||09-07-2000||03:00 PM||||Im almost done finishing the drywall in my garage, and just have one side of the garage to paint.<P>Im going to buy those epoxy concrete paints.  I was wondering if anyone has done this before and what is the best paint.  <P>Behr sells concreat cleaner plus a concrete etcher and rust remover.<P>Im planning on doing half of the garage with two coats and doing the other half a week later.<P>Rustoleum sells an epoxy floor coating for 89 bucks.  two component heavy duty epoxy coating, withstands heavyduty foot and vehicle traffic.  Covers 625 feet, at 5mils thickness, good thing i think?<BR>dries  to traffic in 4 days<P>behr 1 part epoxy acrylic 23 bucks.<BR>resists hot tire pickup, chemicals, water, oil, and gasoline.  designed to protect, durable, water repellent.<P>I dont know what to use, and im afraid of it scratcing or something peeling the paint.  The rustoleum seems thicker, and the behr, suggests it resists the hot tire pickup, oil and gasoline.<P>if anyone has suggestions<BR>thank you<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-07-2000||04:14 PM||||You have to be really careful with some of this stuff for the floor.  It can get really slick when wet. <P>On the walls. If they are below ground use Drylock on them. That stuff will seal them good.<P>On the floor you might want to consider just a sealer so stuff can't soak in. <P>Depends alot on how much work you will do in there. The sealers soak into the concrete pretty much but anything that adds a skin is going to get chewed up over time. Point loads from jacks and jack stands can be ammazingly high. Figure you are going to lift one end of a Fiero that's about a 1000-1200 pound load. A floor jacks wheels have maybe the contact area of a postage stamp. That's allot differant then 1000-1200 pounds sitting on tires.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LAMBO||09-07-2000||05:19 PM||||DO NOT buy the cheap stuff! You'll be sorry. The only stuff I've ever seen hold up from bubbling is the paint that Griot's Garage sells. And yes, it ain't cheap, $57-60 a gallon. If you're just looking to seal your floor, use Chem-Seal clear coating. It's cheaper and is very durable. Doesn't get slick when wet either (we use it on our retail greenhouse floors which are wet most of the time.) If you want a source for it, let me know and I'll point you in the right direction.<BR>Lambo||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fret Naught||09-07-2000||07:25 PM||||Time for my two cents worth, I just finished painting the floor of my wood shop, used OIL BASED deck paint from Wal-Mart. I tried some water based floor paint from Home Depot and did not like it at all it dried too flat. The real key to getting a good bond is floor preperation. You don't need all that stuff from the store. It is essentially all the same stuff. Go to your local pool/spa store and get a gallon of muratic acid mix it with water at a ratio of 4:1 4 parts water 1 part acid. This does a great job of degreasing and etching the concrete so that it will bond.<BR>Things to remember new concrete requires time to age prior to painting. If you have lots of drywall dust mud etc... on the floor scrub and clean real good first with TSP and water. As always wear rubber gloves and eye protection. Practice safe work habits when using the washes. I did this at my last house, and the paint held up to normal wear the whole time I was there 7 years. But even at that 7 years is a long time. Also if you are worried about it being slick (and it is) stir some sand into your paint. I didn't do this because I want to be able to clean it easy, and I figured the rough texture would make it harder.  Have fun, I hated every minute of my job!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000004||theogre||09-08-2000||12:19 AM||||This is new concrete? It should be left awhile before doing stuff like this.<P>If you do the acid thing be careful. real careful. wear rubber gloves rubber boots and eye protection. make sure you leave the door open when you do it. I remember it not smelling very good. Read the container. Keep a hose handy in case you get it on you or something else you value.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||09-08-2000||03:38 AM||||im pretty sure the garage got built with the house about 21-22 years ago.<P>The electrician, that done my wiring, told me that he painted his concrete, and he used that grit to make the floor non skid.  He told me not to use it if i plan on mopping because it tears the mop up pretty quick.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierospeeder||09-08-2000||05:18 AM||||i just found this company that is pretty popular with the automotive industry with concrete epoxy.  <P>There was a link to them from the crank and chrome website.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ucoatit.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ucoatit.com</A> <BR>the kit is about 250 bucks and its a 3 part setup.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fret Naught||09-09-2000||02:40 PM||||I know that the acid thing sucks, but every product I looked at required etching prior to paint to insure good adheasion. With a 4:1 dilution ratio it is not as bad as you would think, but as always wear the proper clothing, and take precautions.||||reg||1
Z||000008||peterh||09-12-2000||02:34 PM||||My experience: I painted my old garage floor last year with Sears polyurathane and added their sand addin. I picked white trying to lighten up the garage, what a mistake. The floor looks worse than when I started it as the white got real dirty. The noskid stuff makes it hard to clean up and the paint is lifting in spots even though I washed the floor with a degraser. I will be repainting in the fall with a grey garage floor paint. Hope it works better this time.<BR>||||reg||1
